      "id": "issue-007",
      "number": "007",
      "date": "OCTOBER 2026",
      "title": "THE BEAUTY OF AUTUMN",
      "subtitle": "Berry lips, brown eyeliner, the return of red lipstick and the little rituals that make getting ready half the fun.",
      "cover": "https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/october-cover.jpeg",
      "articles": [
        {
          "category": "BEAUTY",
          "title": "The Autumn Beauty Edit",
          "excerpt": "A darker lip, a little brown eyeliner and makeup that doesn’t need to look perfect.",
          "image": "https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/5zWe5k9u5paGTqnPiMjlaA9q3I_564d404qB8t6qrq7F_ScMvzzbsz9sT-TtqqJtfJ7t4fi18a8HKmKfN2fR0HM-MJ4ONC3zJINm6k_k16wmJxe4QOsla1fiCY-i0KUaseXkkwPfnzkpOVI2GeML8vI6erzQFec-djH7wnZOCHKZuupePqXvxWMmBn29Bn0V.jpeg",
          "body": "The Autumn Beauty Edit\n\nAutumn makeup is always a little more fun. After months of sunscreen, tinted moisturiser and whatever lip product was at the bottom of a beach bag, a darker lipstick suddenly feels right again.\n\nThis season, the shades to watch are cherry, wine and soft brown. Not necessarily worn all at once, and definitely not with the idea that everyone needs to buy an entirely new makeup bag. A berry stain tapped onto the lips can do more for an outfit than a complicated routine.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/0w4WM6_8tz_JVU4akXkE27QVV6boD34Lfciy7RYUKCqmj9eXWTF2R0_UZrVp-cgG0aJz13m3UZ_qGj3Rmvp88lWC_1A4KXIRReEBz0YzYn6JTj_IfLmSquOnE9TLAG5BKEqci83GKCI1zHFxOid3b_vbXlnuk_rLG4rRGgRs_WsoUBpCT21qLbJP13pt5S2T.jpeg]]\n\nThe 1990s references are still there, especially in the softly smudged brown eyeliner and neutral shadows. Think less perfect smoky eye, more makeup that looks even better a few hours after you put it on. A little colour close to the lash line, brushed-out brows and skin that still looks like skin are enough.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/bVWiDmM5TbC9qB_QiQjuyXktF2KOSR25R4VIRwmMi1dQkq9r1w3L2eSIVe_nZvEoP4rdt9XRDAoBFqG7ineyypJy2XciHd03LpmxtkWHKoreA5H-Nn3IbmG-kDAb8HCJDO2w0iNFZuDjr-Shu1NxTGjvfGSfu7w-Yf29WOyLZKq-DCWFULcP2EBpoFTpCJuE.jpeg]]\n\nThere's something refreshing about leaving texture alone, too. Freckles don't need covering, blush doesn't need to be perfectly placed, and a bit of shine isn't the end of the world. The best autumn looks have a slightly lived-in quality.\n\nA deep red manicure or glossy, loosely styled hair fits the mood without demanding much effort. That's probably the nicest thing about the season's beauty direction: you can try a new shade, keep everything else familiar and still feel like you've changed things up."
        },
        {
          "category": "BEAUTY & CULTURE",
          "title": "The Return of the Red Lip",
          "excerpt": "From Old Hollywood to today’s blurred berry stains, there’s more than one way to wear red.",
          "image": "https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/NgIiSMt-xDKNOPnsmCv174kBGVXpPBJppmKrOSLSjFWuLloY0l70dnznJ_07d_HMjhHDyN0NSAzOXyUgy2p6J5F8Zht1qXgGcviSBMno-6dllsqKg2qEFOnSO2_yYLWFfBybVXGQ_dkvJplXsh00dOxyNaw1AK4bMWJot2I2IFpxozDC89svSD6BHQp7hvUm.jpeg",
          "body": "The Return of the Red Lip\n\nA red lipstick has a way of making an ordinary outfit feel intentional. White shirt, jeans, hair pulled back, red lip. Suddenly it looks like you planned the whole thing.\n\nThe look has a long history, of course. Marilyn Monroe made bright red part of her Hollywood image, while the supermodels of the 1990s often went darker, pairing wine-toned lips with barely-there eyes or a smudged liner. Both versions still work, which says quite a lot about a colour that fashion keeps trying to reinvent.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/arlZpomvIhtXKT3Oi3Sokb5m1Z7M1OongB5RzfAxlI7YWiDVMv4I0kdhEpHr-xpRtpFHU6qxbmiVNTr06s5IuN5S-rzntuqMHe4UHYcfKumjtDxlefN2IKtQ2DRG5JW-VEHUUjgXFVeYIIFN36KmOBKBsEkZTOtspF--LhkAfXlg-fau6igVjO4fcwO5By6y.jpeg]]\n\nLately, I've been drawn to the softer interpretation: lipstick pressed on with a fingertip rather than applied in a perfect outline. Makeup artist Nina Park's blurred-lip technique has helped bring that look back into focus. It feels less formal, especially in berry and cranberry shades.\n\nBut there's room for the classic version too. A carefully lined crimson lip with clean skin and mascara has its own appeal. It's the kind of makeup that doesn't need much company.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/vRIaNPcpzUqQvia8zgE4NOunRngywv7jsKCDDjN7e971v5w5-7GTxQeT9DywYH2CfVKJT8GoJXxojXZn8Dvm_irHlSN5HdnD2PFVyYne2ByMp8seBRi7qsJ1Qr3Zj4ccZtcatcfph8mN411weTTLMaVTYEyMK2C0GUz70jQ2PYVhALHGHaKlkhT1G9933Bfw.jpeg]]\n\nThe trick is finding the red you actually want to wear. Some people love a bright, almost orange-red. Others prefer a deep cherry that could pass for their natural lip colour in dim light. There isn't a universally flattering answer, and that's what makes trying them on half the fun.\n\nRed lipstick doesn't have to be saved for a special occasion. Sometimes the occasion is simply getting dressed."
        },
        {
          "category": "LIFESTYLE",
          "title": "The Ritual of Getting Ready",
          "excerpt": "The playlist, the perfume, the outfit changes. Sometimes the best part happens before you leave.",
          "image": "https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/4NuV4hWO3lG7-cH4jAprEi8yXForyT2ugUTlGKkYi7mRYxSyUMp9pIchvd9sCCW9WjawyAblkV3kZwXVcuvWv5ToHL6-Z3MtEVZHxaBoq1K70h76ZdglIYNZvHnXCvImAuL4wkZxb8hQP6NxEWdIIJAjLz9JMSeFw6MUNiVgkV8KcjRj41TQQHr6GiKcR0CU.jpeg",
          "body": "The Ritual of Getting Ready\n\nSometimes getting ready is better than the actual plan. There's music playing, three outfits on the bed, and someone texting to ask why you're still at home. Somehow, it's all part of the evening.\n\nI love the small rituals: choosing a perfume, putting on jewellery, deciding whether the hair looks better up or down. None of it is particularly important on its own, but together it turns an ordinary Friday into something to look forward to.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/uYAMC0CZCDDDAJaIrg3EirmhthTTg0z-jINDTN_cupH6RwvE62S0zRiKeqKP8qiYftQAQOIizbXy01U1lXbYWIHyN8a_sBo7sAAkE9GujwLMN9An6CN7wFSn1r895z3AmyFhzfFkNzw2m_5kuf0FatEfkYyBP4_SNcsddRECWLnKEr87-yIPQT-ITtcv8KlO.jpeg]]\n\nThe dressing table has changed over the years. What once held powder compacts and silver brushes now shares space with phone chargers, lip liners and an open Pinterest board. The impulse is much the same, though. Getting ready gives us a little time to decide how we want to feel.\n\nFragrance is usually the final detail. Some perfumes belong to particular seasons, others to particular people. One spray can bring back a holiday, a dinner or a night you haven't thought about in ages.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/FDGH8hjmIR93O81QO5dTLTI7kcd41MkZkGfYwc1y9F0t9X1KbN95xWbtTQL8E-UlRH8-Zux6uL5Fk3OmmaZ8f5JCTPoZQsa7FiPoV1x4fxniAkPyf2WYqBV4Y22f8beEqR2rc2ItZoPUidMpX-JVDjZnoQoq7Q8jfpvmvz9fJAEwGckZTEBpN-IP5HZ16Q8U.jpeg]]\n\nAnd getting ready with friends deserves its own category. Borrowed makeup, last-minute outfit swaps, someone insisting the first shoes were better. Those are often the moments that make it into the camera roll.\n\nNot every day needs a full routine. But there's something nice about taking ten extra minutes for yourself, even when the only plan is dinner close to home."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "issue-006",
      "number": "006",
      "date": "SEPTEMBER 2026",
      "title": "THE RIVIERA",
      "subtitle": "A last look at the Riviera: Italian afternoons, Slim Aarons photographs and the summer clothes we keep coming back to.",
      "cover": "https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/SlimAaronsStyle_p098a.avif",
      "articles": [
        {
          "category": "LIFESTYLE",
          "title": "The Art of the Italian Summer",
          "excerpt": "Long lunches, little harbours and the simple pleasures that make Italian summers hard to forget.",
          "image": "https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/qGxKTa4A-NWXGWT3-aKyAoMWxm8pNTzrXAhSR7Y2TfBdc3nwzT_Pl6Iz3WnMF6QVReow7KvU9-3sf6ZmBom3mvbC4Y3jn0r6g_X0BxyryZvTFMAQKI1-jU05R-X33buIgJ-AgWjyNZugpZF9bacPHaNXNmdlseUjhbxp4oY0Yao3f_MBRbVBbL9JJ-_urVyT.jpeg",
          "body": "The Art of the Italian Summer\n\nAn Italian summer is made up of tiny details: the first coffee of the morning, towels hanging from balconies, boats nudging against the harbour and the question of where to have lunch taking up most of the afternoon.\n\nPortofino looks almost unreal in photographs, with its pastel buildings crowded around the water. Capri has a different energy: busy little streets, dramatic cliffs, and sunglasses that somehow feel essential even in the shade. Both places have spent decades attracting people who want a particular version of summer.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/2mdB8CW00GSawB01KDRx74RFBfS65I9wVXEL6bhGrO1mHTl-EslRde6-MlJHCOwjrJ9yKUX6XNl-FI03JYc4ui_9gCE-pEa3Axh3cBrXM4MHsoXUpNwek1YNsRdG_w3yZh-JvaKPHybXfJKCgk9RtIVhpjNRCILdsb9XLw_Fwj5DCnSGnY944Pedm2VTtxnL.jpeg]]\n\nThe glamorous image has a history. During the middle of the twentieth century, Mediterranean resorts became gathering places for actors, artists and international travellers. Photographs of silk scarves, white trousers and long lunches helped build the fantasy we still associate with the Riviera.\n\nBut the best part of *la dolce vita* isn't really the glamour. It's the permission to enjoy an ordinary day. A late breakfast becomes a ritual. Lunch runs longer than expected. Nobody seems particularly concerned about checking the time.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/pfi3Vv4xFGvxmYJCRFPDYmTvfa1ViuABDjYb7b5yT4830qcS-ZzCgv4TlHCVVprhI_hj2a7K6nxvmWht65VF2GR5krrJZLA9HQpawJyMPlVoSqzEiafFkQy5P4mq5pX6KUwqFYY0PMS7aZvhP_9AY4oqswSaJvsljlA4rXywFu2YLXP9V5tzUGx2gdUDx4xm.jpeg]]\n\nThe clothes fit that rhythm: linen shirts, simple dresses, woven sandals and a scarf that works just as well in your hair as around your neck.\n\nIt's easy to romanticise the Italian summer, especially from somewhere else. Still, there's a good reason those images keep coming back. They remind us that a memorable day doesn't always need a complicated plan."
        },
        {
          "category": "CULTURE",
          "title": "Slim Aarons: Photographing the Beautiful Life",
          "excerpt": "Pools, parasols and impossibly beautiful afternoons through the lens of Slim Aarons.",
          "image": "https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/n1qkBj-c2-oqgHpWtD-iDuN5FopW-YiTM9NdmI3njazM2uv-WvwWf8FhxZiLdf2oFZcyLpzhMRVireHIT1I6I1yl7QQts6Mz2yinb5vPS0_xDVoHsjn-eBUWw69srNOd2eEoXyxNTZrK15QxiW3vmdmMzAGbFrLO1z-M1VqqmH8q1uc0QyGZVu0xJQN010XM.jpeg",
          "body": "Slim Aarons: Photographing the Beautiful Life\n\nIf you've ever saved a photograph of a perfect poolside terrace, there's a good chance Slim Aarons had something to do with the way you imagine it.\n\nHis pictures are full of colour, sunlight and people who appear to have nowhere urgent to be. There are striped umbrellas, impossibly blue swimming pools, beautiful houses and guests dressed as though a photographer just happened to catch them looking their best.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/e482cd1Cj3Zit1ZVl0i30zSudIbniI2zxs4cUu70BWy1Btvs2JdH7nyOp0t2XxYr_7p98sltEx2IcmoFJm3Uqo5YVDxDcGCVKWU9j4CcoiAnlI0Njks9gmwZaGONrMzM4QJfUjj5cu61vFQUAXb803DoL56yRplIyQ9PqpmcWJU2uV_Fl8SyFBBxCKhfJ7qG.jpeg]]\n\nAarons began his career photographing war, before turning his camera towards the social world of the postwar decades. By the 1950s and 1960s, he was documenting holidays and private gatherings in places such as Capri, Palm Springs and the French Riviera.\n\nHis 1970 photograph *Poolside Gossip* is a good place to start. Taken at the Kaufmann Desert House, it shows an almost absurdly perfect meeting of architecture, fashion and afternoon light. Everything looks relaxed, even though the composition is carefully balanced.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/yARnB97cdEDSdh9GZFx56noOjIakGHnq1J-pyUzQjpTT0_uD_tvP5165kPIOgww7aQvdvlkIz2BXMvys49s5KdOLDJJr8A9tAl3xfpWHspQ-8-LV5bOQx0vwLVR5SdioJf2y7KeieCTGoJsj9WEXlSk8Ub9A8-qDrOkSJFgamSoc0Vdd8Z5-bN2TrZIiLRlW.jpeg]]\n\nThat's what makes his work interesting. The photographs feel spontaneous, but they're also incredibly precise. The architecture frames the people; the colours lead your eye; even the empty space matters.\n\nToday, the influence is everywhere, from resort campaigns to hotel interiors and the vintage photographs pinned to summer moodboards. The lifestyle he captured was exclusive, not an everyday reality for most people. But the pictures still have a pull. They're less about a destination than the feeling of being somewhere you wish the afternoon would never end."
        },
        {
          "category": "FASHION",
          "title": "The Riviera Wardrobe: An Enduring Elegance",
          "excerpt": "Bardot, Jackie Kennedy and the summer pieces that never really leave our wardrobes.",
          "image": "https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/riviera-bardot.jpeg",
          "body": "The Riviera Wardrobe: An Enduring Elegance\n\nThe best summer outfits rarely involve very much. A white dress, flat sandals, a basket bag and good sunglasses can take you from breakfast to dinner without a second thought.\n\nThat easy approach to dressing is part of what made the French and Italian Rivieras so influential. In the 1950s and 1960s, women visiting Saint-Tropez and Capri began to look different from the way they dressed in the city. Clothes were lighter, silhouettes more relaxed and practicality suddenly looked chic.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/r9_JdLWUn6wBlyuxmrWy61FTFjppAlF1VCJW6gaXaztQCYIRPqN4Rwc_jsJN2P0LvFHVQumOA_c3sm8XxklzRVcfpJRurCC_PWzlJrsftU0-RygGuLSTWR7cyu2P1xjHOYG9ZhOoeIPARVMI7vw3bcg3RqTWn3ToOi6pUrTSXrXX1UwXlKf0C6jbtCwyaxM8.jpeg]]\n\nBrigitte Bardot's gingham dresses and ballet flats became part of Saint-Tropez's visual identity. Jackie Kennedy offered another version in Capri: cropped trousers, silk scarves and oversized sunglasses. Their wardrobes weren't identical, but both showed how much personality can fit into simple pieces.\n\nThe fabrics matter. Cotton and linen feel right in the heat, and a silk scarf can make an outfit look finished without making it fussy. A little gold jewellery, a well-worn pair of sandals and a bag you don't mind carrying all day do the rest.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/ZnMAofYEYkobADOE-wHzXHTm-vWAW_4-AtDiKX_BO-L09P4wB6EUGqdGy-pqhSy5Ak-igSUlMcpp5fEtgA54W1c7TtORiBNZXw6hYqoPCiSrWAXXifE3hf0zZ1P3wHvonrpyP93Se0kTUizSzhM3oVmgsX9Vnxf4eDISKcx1tYjIGFhUavsf87yFag-tEpra.jpeg]]\n\nWhat I like most about Riviera style is that it doesn't need to look new. A dress you've worn every summer, a vintage scarf or your favourite sunglasses often works better than an outfit bought for one photograph.\n\nIt's summer dressing at its easiest, which is probably why we keep returning to it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "issue-005",
      "number": "005",
      "date": "AUGUST 2026",
      "title": "THE JEWELLERY",
      "subtitle": "Cartier panthers, favourite heirlooms and the women who made some of history’s most memorable jewels their own.",
      "cover": "https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/marisa-berenson-photo-henry-clarke.jpg",
      "articles": [
        {
          "category": "JEWELLERY",
          "title": "The Cartier Panthère: An Icon of Timeless Luxury",
          "excerpt": "How Jeanne Toussaint turned a wild animal into Cartier’s most recognisable character.",
          "image": "https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/Cartier-Panther.jpg",
          "body": "The Cartier Panthère: An Icon of Timeless Luxury\n\nA panther isn't the most obvious choice for a jewellery house. It's wild, a little intimidating and not particularly interested in looking delicate. Maybe that's why Cartier's version has lasted so long.\n\nThe motif appeared in Cartier's world in 1914, first through spotted patterns and illustrations. But it was Jeanne Toussaint, who became artistic director of the house's high jewellery department in 1933, who really gave the animal its personality.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/CN00034792.webp]]\n\nUnder her direction, panthers began to climb across brooches, curl around bracelets and perch on extraordinary gemstones. They looked alert and almost alive, rather than simply decorative.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/Cartier-Panther.jpg]]\n\nOne of the best-known examples was made for the Duchess of Windsor in 1949: a diamond-and-sapphire panther resting above a large sapphire. It's dramatic, certainly, but also surprisingly playful. The animal seems ready to move.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/wilT6DJwqholwMECUL-gjtsV9QuqFVDRygP_veJ_t47eifNLyxV7YL53_XDP0uLswpm-UBYqgLw-M37sGet5Bgo1WPRxSrH1lq3IGQJ2wDwsYf2Xe_iw7eftnLz_96M2djLbL2rVVQu17SuhA2e_shkB7OVSjGIgulu7QFXLy2y8aZA2c367SDh9MtrlYOI2.jpeg]]\n\nLook closely at a Panthère piece and the workmanship becomes the story. Tiny stones create the spotted coat, a pair of coloured eyes changes the expression, and the angle of a paw can make a rigid piece of jewellery feel animated.\n\nCartier has returned to the panther again and again, from realistic sculptural designs to simpler outlines. The details change, but the attitude doesn't. It remains jewellery for someone who doesn't mind being noticed."
        },
        {
          "category": "JEWELLERY",
          "title": "The Art of the Heirloom",
          "excerpt": "On lucky charms, Van Cleef & Arpels and the jewellery we hold on to for reasons beyond its value.",
          "image": "https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/francoisehardy-1-1024x802.jpg",
          "body": "The Art of the Heirloom\n\nMy favourite thing about old jewellery is that it comes with a past. A ring might have been worn to a wedding, a necklace kept for special dinners, a bracelet put on every morning for years. Even when you don't know the full story, you can feel that it has one.\n\nVan Cleef & Arpels understands the appeal of jewellery with meaning. Founded in Paris in 1906, the house has long drawn on flowers, butterflies, good-luck symbols and other small details from nature.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/francoisehardy-1-1024x802.jpg]]\n\nThe Alhambra collection, introduced in 1968, is perhaps its most familiar design. The original long necklace featured twenty clover-inspired motifs, framed with tiny golden beads. It was simple enough to wear every day, but distinctive enough to recognise instantly.\n\nThe clover wasn't chosen at random. Jacques Arpels was known for collecting four-leaf clovers and giving them to people as little wishes for good fortune. It's a lovely idea to carry into a piece of jewellery.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/0bc70269a0a43dcaed143b06d008a023.jpg]]\n\nThe design has since appeared in mother-of-pearl, onyx, malachite and other materials. Some versions are quiet; others are much more colourful. The shape stays familiar, even as the pieces take on different personalities.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/iMPupAVpTF83pe4kfN9Jhi-1920-80.jpg.webp]]\n\nOf course, an heirloom doesn't need a famous name or an expensive stone. The most meaningful pieces are often the ones you associate with someone. A small pendant from a grandmother can matter far more than something valuable bought yesterday.\n\nThat's what makes jewellery different from so many other things we own. It can outlast a trend, travel between generations and still feel personal to the next person who wears it."
        },
        {
          "category": "JEWELLERY",
          "title": "Diamonds, Icons & the Women Who Wore Them",
          "excerpt": "Elizabeth Taylor, Grace Kelly and Princess Diana each had their own way of wearing extraordinary jewels.",
          "image": "https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/elizabeth_taylore_wearing_bulgari_emerald_jewellery.jpg__1536x0_q75_crop-scale_subsampling-2_upscale-false.jpg",
          "body": "Diamonds, Icons & the Women Who Wore Them\n\nSome jewellery is famous because of the stone. Other pieces are remembered because of the person wearing it. Elizabeth Taylor, Grace Kelly and Princess Diana understood that distinction better than most.\n\nTaylor loved jewellery openly and enthusiastically. Her collection included the 33.19-carat Krupp Diamond, given to her by Richard Burton in 1968, and the historic La Peregrina pearl. She didn't treat these pieces like museum objects. She wore them, talked about them and clearly enjoyed them.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/elizabeth_taylore_wearing_bulgari_emerald_jewellery.jpg__1536x0_q75_crop-scale_subsampling-2_upscale-false.jpg]]\n\nGrace Kelly's approach was quieter. Her Cartier engagement ring, with its approximately 10.47-carat emerald-cut diamond, was spectacular, but it suited her restrained style. Even with royal jewellery, she had a way of letting the clothes, the jewels and the person work together.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/DXzBdOpKk8gYgrD1VzAVwwRXCWCF35nKVAKvRr5iM35sPzAMRx8dAV11bwY3-UvA-quz0suvhPjSBK4HhGBH6xDdJLwyjdjypKIUV7Ry4BcESAviCtDDkJbGDcTBM3otB14vozBR3pv3niD-bzxf0vuXeAe3FYkCR5Cgrz8GKqveIEGnah5F4_QVyY6raxm6.jpeg]]\n\nDiana brought something else: a willingness to experiment. Her sapphire engagement ring became one of the most recognisable pieces of royal jewellery, but I find her less conventional choices just as interesting. In 1985, she wore an emerald-and-diamond choker across her forehead during a visit to Australia. It was unexpected and completely memorable.\n\n[[image:https://maisonverve.github.io/maison-verve/ZHP-KVRPfWKc0xQ-6DCBRrs6yK7OyHR535DkUjUgQOM4UJqIDKPGtLCx4IcGsVhm1LaS8NKscJ1QP-jpaa1Zbj8I-SXPCt01gWvKrWlosndrvJxSUTYZnwWsWFR4k2RHLPW1pfblB6gMRvuFZQuaOzzE2sNaiDYyC82rA1aT26pi0izBTH35LaJfd6TZnNgX.jpeg]]\n\nThese women didn't have the same taste. Taylor loved theatrical glamour, Kelly favoured restraint and Diana often challenged the rules. That's precisely why their jewellery photographs remain so interesting.\n\nThe stones may be extraordinary, but it's the styling, the occasion and the person behind each piece that make the images worth revisiting."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
